//! check: fail(E1015)
//! phase: typecheck
//! conforms: mem.str.view.lend
//!
//! s89's soundness guard (wolf-lang#86). `s.bytes()` in an argument
//! position LENDS the string's own storage — that is what makes the
//! call free — and a lend that outlives the call it was scoped to is a
//! dangling `{ptr, len}`, the fast version of no answer at all.
//!
//! `keep` returns its parameter, so the bytes would leave with it.
//! Keeping that out is the checker's job and this is the witness that
//! it does it: the error lands on the LEND, names the use that would
//! outlive it, and prescribes the one-word fix — bind the bytes first
//! (`let bs = s.bytes()`), because a `let` materializes and the bound
//! list is an ordinary value the callee may own.
//!
//! The rule is deliberately narrow. A callee whose use of the parameter
//! this analysis cannot classify is NOT an error: the caller
//! materializes for it, exactly as every caller did before views
//! crossed calls. Only a provable escape is refused, so the diagnostic
//! never stands between a program and a meaning it already had.
//!
//! Its sibling `corpus/strings/byte_view_lend.lu` is the accepting
//! half: the same call shape, with callees that only read.

fn keep(bs: List[int]) -> List[int] {
    bs
}

fn main() -> !int {
    let s = "wolf"
    let held = keep(s.bytes())     // ERROR E1015: the lend outlives the call
    print("{held.len}")
    0
}
